{
  "version": 1,
  "vocabVersion": "consensus-vocab-1",
  "note": "The reader's words, mapped onto the 59 closed attributes. consensus-vocab.json's gloss is written for the attributor; questioner-phrasings.json turns each attribute into a question; this file is the third face of the same claim — what a person TYPES. It is the most dangerous file in the search for the same reason questioner-phrasings.json is the most dangerous file in the questioner: a wrong entry makes the reader search for something other than what they said, and nothing downstream can detect it. Every phrase below was written against the gloss AND the NOT clause, and a phrase was left out whenever the NOT clause said it would be a lie.",
  "rules": [
    "A phrase earns its place only if a reader typing it means the gloss. 'atmospheric' means nothing in this vocabulary and is deliberately absent — it would have to guess between menacing, dreamlike, austere and painterly, and guessing is how a search stops being trustworthy.",
    "Ambiguity is expressed, not resolved. 'fast' is genuinely relentless-or-brisk, so it maps to BOTH and becomes one anyOf clause. It does not silently pick one.",
    "`polarity: \"inherent\"` marks a phrase that already contains its own absence — 'little dialogue', 'no dialogue', 'barely speak'. A preceding 'not' must not invert it a second time, and the parser refuses to.",
    "A phrase must not be a plot word. 'someone dies' is not subject:grief and 'a priest' is not subject:spiritual; the NOT clauses say so explicitly and the lexicon obeys them.",
    "Multi-word phrases are matched longest-first, so 'little dialogue' beats 'dialogue' and 'coming of age' beats 'coming'."
  ],
